            <ArticleTitle>Development of Cell-Specific Aptamers: Recent Advances and Insight into the Selection Procedures.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">E2070</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3390/molecules22122070</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Systematic evolution of ligands by exponential enrichment (SELEX) is an established procedure for developing short single-stranded nucleic acid ligands called aptamers against a target of choice. This approach has also been used for developing aptamers specific to whole cells named Cell-SELEX. Aptamers selected by Cell-SELEX have the potential to act as cell specific therapeutics, cell specific markers or cell specific drug delivery and imaging agents. However, aptamer development is a laborious and time-consuming process which is often challenging due to the requirement of frequent optimization of various steps involved in Cell-SELEX procedures. This review provides an insight into various procedures for selection, aptamer enrichment, regeneration and aptamer-binding analysis, in addition to a very recent update on all aptamers selected by Cell-SELEX procedures.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
